Use of Switch: 9/10 - Shared rooms, scoped addressing, authenticated approval cards and event streams enforce the central disclosure workflow; the offline stand-in’s limitations are clearly distinguished.

Technical execution: 8/10 - Concrete cross-platform commands, restart tests, tamper checks and reported second-machine reproduction support credibility, but the actual two-owner, two-machine workflow remains untested.

Creativity and impact: 9/10 - Selectively questioning another team’s sealed coding session addresses a specific review problem, combining verifiable evidence with author-controlled disclosure.

Presentation and documentation: 8/10 - The opening scenario, transcript, diagram and explicit limits explain the product well, although substantial protocol terminology and operational detail make the README demanding.

Shortlist: yes - This is a distinctive, technically credible workflow with a clear reason to depend on Switch and unusually candid boundaries.

The three weaknesses that cost the most points:
1. The central cross-team deployment has not been demonstrated with author and reviewer on separate machines; local reproduction does not establish that full operating model.
2. Human participation remains awkward: Slack questions are ignored, answers require the gateway, and separate logins and join-line exchanges add adoption friction.
3. Practical review coverage is narrow: only Claude Code sessions are supported, questions and contradiction checks use fixed rules, and valid proofs cannot establish session authenticity before sealing or answer completeness.

TOTAL: 8.6
